# Stalwart environment, read by stalwart.service. See
# https://stalw.art/docs/configuration/environment-variables/
#
# Stalwart is served at the root of its own host name through Traefik (it
# cannot be mounted under a path like Radicale's /calendar). The public URL
# is what discovery documents advertise and what the WebUI accepts sign-in
# for; it must match what clients use.
STALWART_HOSTNAME=calendar.vinkies.net
STALWART_PUBLIC_URL=https://calendar.vinkies.net

# First boot only: there is no /etc/stalwart/config.json yet, so Stalwart
# starts in bootstrap mode and serves its setup wizard on plain HTTP port
# 8080, on all interfaces and without password-guessing protection. Complete
# it at http://<hub>:8080/admin (from another machine, tunnel it with
# `ssh -L 8080:localhost:8080 <hub>`), then restart the service:
#   systemctl restart stalwart
# The wizard writes config.json to /etc/stalwart and the RocksDB data store
# under /var/lib/stalwart; the daily duplicity backup covers both.
# In the wizard, leave "Automatically Obtain TLS Certificate" off: Traefik
# already terminates TLS for calendar.vinkies.net and holds port 443.
#
# The wizard creates Stalwart's default listeners: SMTP 25/465, IMAP 993,
# POP3 995, ManageSieve 4190, HTTPS 443 and HTTP 8080. Port 443 belongs to
# Traefik, so that listener fails to bind (logged as a registry.build-error,
# harmless). In the WebUI, remove the HTTPS and mail listeners if the hub is
# only used for calendars/contacts, and change the http listener's bind
# address to 127.0.0.1:8080 so only Traefik can reach it. Listener changes
# take effect after a restart.
#
# Calendar/contact URLs after setup (account is the full email address):
#   https://calendar.vinkies.net/dav/cal/<account>/
#   https://calendar.vinkies.net/dav/card/<account>/
